            '- green means that data are already present,\n' ...
            '- light green arrow means presence of secondary data overshadowed by data \n' ...
            'of the other arrow (can be made primary by selection),\n' ...
            '- dark green marks an arrow with directly loaded data,\n' ...
            '- cyan is the color of a currently opened block.\n' ...
            '\n' ...
            'In the graphs with frequency domain data, which open up later, the general \n' ...
            'rules are as follows. Every quantity is shown by defaults in dB (except \n' ...
            'for phase diagrams (degrees) and explicitly denoted linear-in-amplitude \n' ...
            'plots)\n' ...
            '- green + marks denote the measured data\n' ...
            '- yellow x marks denote the variances\n' ...
            '- red line denotes the estimated transfer function\n' ...
            '- cyan is the color of the residuals (the difference between the measured \n' ...
            'data and the estimated transfer function)\n' ...
            '\n' ...
            '!@!Blocks!@!\n' ...
            'Blocks are the key action elements. Only blue or green blocks can be \n' ...
            'opened, by double-click (or right button click). Blocks are in general \n' ...
            'allowed for use if their necessary input data are available. An exception \n' ...
            'is the block "Excitation Signal Design" which also needs for use that \n' ...
            '"UserLevel" is set to "Interactive" or higher. \n' ...
            '\n' ...
            'At start only the blocks "Read Time Domain Data" and "Read Freq. Domain \n' ...
            'Data" can be opened.\n' ...
            '\n' ...
            'Blocks return output data after the Close command. In this case their \n' ...
            'color becomes green. The Cancel command closes them without affecting \n' ...
            'either their output or their previous setting.\n' ...
            '\n' ...
            'When a block is already opened, and we click on another one, a warning \n' ...
