case 'excitation_main'
   switch ObjTag
   case {'excitation_main'}
      Name='Excitation Signal Design';
      LongHelp=sprintf( ...
         [ ...
            'This is the window for the design of different periodic excitation \n' ...
            'signals. Types of signals: multisine, discrete interval binary signal \n' ...
            '(DIBS), discrete interval ternary signal (DITS), or pseudo random binary \n' ...
            'signal (PRBS or MLBS). \n' ...
            '\n' ...
            'The proper design of excitation signals is not easy. Many design \n' ...
            'parameters are interdependent (e.g. the density of the DFT grid, \n' ...
            'determined by the clock frequency and the number of samples: df=fc/N, puts \n' ...
            'an upper limit to the number of harmonics we may have in a given band). \n' ...
            'Therefore \n' ...
            '\n' ...
            '- the value of the clock frequency and the number of samples must be \n' ...
            'carefully chosen, and\n' ...
            '\n' ...
            '- there is some aid in the window to assure consistency: after setting the \n' ...
            'parameters: the buttons "Adjust freqs" or "Adjust samples" set the \n' ...
            'consistency of the design parameters. After having any of them pushed, all \n' ...
            'design parameters will be set properly.\n' ...
            '\n' ...
            'During View and Design the algorithm checks consistency and identification \n' ...
            'relevance of the parameter settings, and\n' ...
            '\n' ...
            '- either gives a warning if they are violated: then you may proceed on \n' ...
            'your own risk,\n' ...
            '\n' ...
            '- or, in more serious cases, an error message is generated: then there is \n' ...
            'no other solution than to manually change the design parameters or/and to \n' ...
            'push either of the "Adjust" buttons.\n' ...
            '\n' ...
            'For PRBS, the number of samples is determined from the given length of the \n' ...
            'shift register; for other signals, it can be explicitly set, and together \n' ...
            'with the clock frequency it determines the DFT grid.\n' ...
            '\n' ...
            'The block allows to either save the designed data through the "File / Save \n' ...
            'signal" menu, as an object (see below) or a vector, or returns output data \n' ...
            'after the Close command. The data are then available on the output arrow, \n' ...
            'either for one of the next blocks (e.g. for simulation), or by \n' ...
            'double-clicking on the arrow, and selecting "Save Arrow Data".\n' ...
            '\n' ...
            'In the resulting object (let us name it ''obj''), the basic data are as \n' ...
            'follows:\n' ...
            '  get(obj) %show all properties\n' ...
            '  obj.u %sample vector\n' ...
            '  obj.Ts %sampling time\n' ...
            '  obj.frequencies %vector of excited frequencies\n' ...
            '\n' ...
            'The Cancel command closes the block without affecting either its output or \n' ...
            'its previous setting.\n' ...
         ]);
      ShortHelp='';
      return
   case {'excitation_menu_file_save_vector'}
      Name='Save signal to vector';
      LongHelp=sprintf( ...
         [ ...
            '!@!Save the signal!@!\n' ...
            'When the signal has been designed, it can be saved through this item as a \n' ...
            'vector.\n' ...
            '\n' ...
            'The contents of the vector is the time domain samples to be downloaded \n' ...
            'into an arbitrary signal generator. The vector in the workspace usually \n' ...
            'needs to be written into an ASCII file, using the command fprintf(...) in a \n' ...
            'cycle (see an example separately).\n' ...
            '!@!Sample program: make ASCII file!@!\n' ...
            'When the samples are in a vector, e.g. ''samples'', a simple program can \n' ...
            'generate a text file for further use. Modify the enclosed commands \n' ...
