case 'fdtool_compinput_freq_main'
   switch ObjTag
   case {'fdtool_compinput_freq_main'}
      Name='Compose Frequency Domain Data';
      LongHelp=sprintf( ...
         [ ...
            '!@!Introduction to Object Composition with GUI!@!\n' ...
            'Compose an fiddata object from workspace variables, given strings, and/or \n' ...
            'from variables in binary file(s), using a question/answer window.\n' ...
            '\n' ...
            'Properties can be set with edit boxes or with popup menus. Edit boxes are \n' ...
            'described in a separate topic, the use of the latter is straightforward.\n' ...
            '\n' ...
            'Obligatory properties are given in red color, recommended (but not \n' ...
            'obligatory) ones in green .\n' ...
            '\n' ...
            'The Compose button executes the object creation, without creating an \n' ...
            'output. If some obligatory fields are not filled in, they flash in red. \n' ...
            'When everything is OK, the Done button saves the object. The menu item \n' ...
            '"File" / "Save object" allow manual saving.\n' ...
            '!@!Data types!@!\n' ...
            'The window allows to import 3 different data types. These can be selected \n' ...
            'using the "Data type" menu.\n' ...
            '\n' ...
            '1) Input/Output data: complex amplitudes in separate column vectors\n' ...
            '2) FRF data, dB/degrees: the data come from a network analyzer, and the \n' ...
            'two available vectors are the gains (magnitudes) in decibels, and the \n' ...
            'phases in degrees\n' ...
            '3) Complex FRF data: column vector of the complex FRF values.\n' ...
            '\n' ...
            'The data can also be cell row vectors with column vectors as elements, or \n' ...
            'arrays with column vectors which are data belonging to different \n' ...
            'experiments.\n' ...
            '\n' ...
            'In each case, the frequency vector must also be provided in the form of a \n' ...
            'variable or a Matlab expression, in Hz.\n' ...
            '!@!Edit boxes!@!\n' ...
            'Edit boxes may contain MATLAB code executable in the main workspace, using \n' ...
            'the variables defined there. Examples:\n' ...
            'var1(:,2)\n' ...
            '[1;2;3]\n' ...
            '''Voltage''\n' ...
            'date\n' ...
            '\n' ...
            'An exception is the field "Notes" which is just a text without quotation \n' ...
            'marks.\n' ...
            '\n' ...
            'An additional possibility is to define the contents of an edit box on the \n' ...
            'basis of a file. To read about this, please select the relevant topic.\n' ...
            '!@!Referring to files!@!\n' ...
            'A file reference can be given in the edit boxes between < > marks, e.g.\n' ...
            '<data.mat>\n' ...
            '<c:\\temp\\data.mat>\n' ...
            '<data.txt>\n' ...
            '\n' ...
            'The "Browse..." buttons do this with the help of the GUI.\n' ...
            '\n' ...
            'For MAT-files, in addition a variable name can be written after the file \n' ...
            'name, separated by a colon:\n' ...
            '<c:\\temp\\data.mat>:freqs\n' ...
            'When there is only one variable in the file, the variable name is not \n' ...
            'needed.\n' ...
            '\n' ...
            'For ASCII files, the load command will be executed on the named ASCII \n' ...
            'file. The result needs to be a MATLAB column vector or an array. Example:\n' ...
            '<data.txt>\n' ...
            'From ASCII files only real data can be imported. Complex data (input and \n' ...
            'output) need to be assembled in the workspace, then they can be imported \n' ...
            'from there.\n' ...
            '\n' ...
            'A subscript can be added to the above described contents of the edit box, \n' ...
            'similarly as to regular MATLAB variables. Examples:\n' ...
            '<data.mat>:freqs(1:20)\n' ...
            '<c:\\temp\\data.txt>(1:8,2)\n' ...
            '\n' ...
         ]);
      ShortHelp='';
      return
   case {'ui_fdidgui_compinp_edit_synch'}
