            'Read tiddata object (or iddata, see the Sysid toolbox) from workspace, or \n' ...
            'from file.\n' ...
            '\n' ...
            'The reading procedure checks the type of the data: they must be objects. \n' ...
            'Such objects can be created by the function "tiddata" from your own \n' ...
            'vectors - see "Help / Getting Started... / Using Time Domain Data", and the \n' ...
            'corresponding function helps for details. The objects allow the use of the \n' ...
            '"get" and "set" functions: e.g. get(dat). In time domain, don''t forget to \n' ...
            'fill in either the "PeriodLength" or the "Frequencies" property, e.g. \n' ...
            '\n' ...
            '     data.periodlength = 0.03;\n' ...
            '\n' ...
            'otherwise unnecessarily long processing time may be necessary later, \n' ...
            'during the execution of the "Variance Analysis" block. If data of more \n' ...
            'than one experiments are used, and measurements are properly synchronized, \n' ...
            'also set the property "synchronization".\n' ...
            '\n' ...
            '!@!Sample program for preparing load!@!\n' ...
            'Let us assume that we have measured five times 1024 time domain samples, \n' ...
            'which are stored in the 1024 x 5 arrays inp and outp. The sampling \n' ...
            'frequency is fs = 40960 Hz, the period length in each signal is 1/160 s \n' ...
            '(there are 4 periods in each record).\n' ...
            '\n' ...
            '%%First prepare the data in 1 x 5 cell arrays:\n' ...
            'u = num2cell(inp,1); y = num2cell(outp,1);\n' ...
            '%%Now make the object:\n' ...
            'obj = tiddata(y,u,1/40960);\n' ...
            '%%Set some properties:\n' ...
            'obj.periodlength = 1/160;\n' ...
            'obj.synchronization=''on'';\n' ...
            'get(obj) %%check properties\n' ...
            '\n' ...
            'Now the variable obj can be imported to this block.\n' ...
            '\n' ...
         ]);
      ShortHelp='';
      return
